--- Finding (hash_join): a function taking a function (keys_of), called with lists
--- of two record kinds, is refused as the spec intends (Specialization: a function
--- taking a function is not copied per kind) — but the refusal misleads twice:
---
--- 1. It names both kinds alike: "its 'rows' is a list of records here where
---    another call makes it a list of records" — the words are Arg::words of each
---    type (lower_ir.rs, the "serves every call" refusal), which drop the record's
---    name, so the two kinds it says differ read the same.
--- 2. A first, cascading mistake blames a correct function: b_of is only ever
---    handed a Q, yet the program is refused at b_of's body with "struct 'P' has
---    no field 'b'" — keys_of was typed by its first call, so the function value
---    b_of was typed as taking a P through it.
---
--- crow run -i and crow run alike (exit 1, nothing printed):
---   pb4.co:8:14: struct 'P' has no field 'b'
---     8 │     return q.b
---   pb4.co:16:1: `keys_of` takes a function, so one `keys_of` serves every call,
---   and its 'rows' is a list of records here where another call makes it a list of
---   records — a function taking a function is not copied per kind (spec,
---   Specialization): write one for each kind
---   2 mistakes
--- Expected: the one refusal at line 16, naming the kinds apart ("a list of Q here
--- where another call makes it a list of P"), and nothing said of b_of's body.
--- (Found writing a hash join that builds its multimap for whichever table is
--- smaller; the solution hashes table B only, so it calls the function once.)

struct P [a]
struct Q [b]

fn a_of[p]
    return p.a

fn b_of[q]
    return q.b

fn keys_of[rows, key]
    out = for r in rows
        key[r]
    return out

log[keys_of[[P[1]], a_of]]
log[keys_of[[Q[2]], b_of]]
